Abstract
In order to accurately and conveniently measure temperature and water content in bio-ethanol, a dual-function ratiometric luminescence sensor has been fabricated successfully based on mixed lanthanide metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) Eu0.02Dy0.18-MOF. In this MOF, the two lanthanides and ligand $\mathrm{(H_{4}L)}$ form three luminescence centers, and they have different sensitivity towards water and temperature. Using the ratio of luminescence intensity at $416~\mathrm{nm}$ and $614~\mathrm{nm}$ , the MOF could quantitatively determine the concentration of water in dry ethanol and in bio-ethanol with recoveries of $91\%\%-107\ \%$ , and a detection limit of $0.1\%$ , which is much lower than in hydrated ethyl alcohol fuel(AEHC), $0.8\%$ in China, $1.0\%$ in the United States, $4.9\%$ in Brazilian. Using the luminescence intensity ratio of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\rightarrow{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{2}$ $\mathrm{614~nm})$ transition to $\mathrm{Dy^{3+}}~{}^{4}\mathrm{F}_{9/2}\rightarrow{}^{6}\mathrm{H}_{13/2}$ 1 $(573\ \mathrm{nm})$ transition, the MOF could also sense temperature in range of $293\mathrm{~K~}$ to $373\mathrm{~K~}$ , and $150~\mathrm{K}$ to $298\mathrm{~K~}$ with high accuracy, excellent linear relationship, reversibility and recyclability.
Keywords：mofs, ratiometric luminescence sensor, bio-ethanol, temperature
## Introduction
Large amount of carbon dioxide emissions caused by burning fossil fuels, and shortage of fossil fuels have been hot topics all over the word. Herein, looking for suitable alternatives is vital and necessary. Bio-ethanol may be an excellent candidate, for it could greatly reduce the consumption of fossil fuels, and release less greenhouse gases, which alleviates the environmental crisis.1,2 Moreover, bio-ethanol derives from sugar cane, corn, wheat, sugar beet, cassava,3,4 and it’s renewable source. But bio-ethanol is highly hygroscopic,5 and could easy to be adulterated with water which is colorless, odorless, and not easily detectable.6 Besides, too much water in ethanol fuel will have a big bad influence on its performance, for example causing engine malfunction.7,8 In fact, the water content in bio-ethanol fuels has been regulated to less than $1.0\%$ in the United States,9 $4.9\%$ in Brazilian,10 and $0.8\%$ in China.11 So detection of water content in ethanol is critical. Karl Fischer titration is the conventional method, but it usually requires dedicated instrumentation and skilled operators, and is high-cost.12-15 Recently, several other methods have also been developed, such as $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR,10 surface plasmon resonance,16 conductometry.17 But compared with those methods, luminescence sensing may be more popular, because it is sensitive, easy to operate, quick response, non-toxic, and recyclable. And recently, some new fluorescent ratiometric sensors for water in ethanol based on porphyrin Zinc,18 fluorescent dyes pyranines,1 $\mathrm{^{\circ}R u}@\mathrm{MIL}.101(\mathrm{Al}){\mathrm{-NH}}_{2},$ 20 carbon dots encapsulated MOF,21 and $\mathrm{T}\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ post-coordinated MOF,22 have been synthesized.
Temperature is a important parameter in scientific research, social construction, and daily life, and accurately measuring it is essential. Luminescence thermometers, a kind of different and new technique, are high accuracy, short response time, high spatial resolution than conventional ones, could resist strong electric and magnetic fields, and realize to detect temperature in biological fluids and fast-moving objects without contact.23, 24 And some related researches based on lanthanide MOFs, quantum dots/dyes encapsulated MOFs, rare-earth doped phosphors and up-converting materials have been reported.25-34
Among those luminescence materials, lanthanides metal organic frameworks (Ln - MOFs) have attracted more attention. Since they usually have sharp emission peaks, strong luminescence intensity, wide spectrum scope,35 and multiple emission centers that include metal ions, organic ligands, and guest molecules in the structure. Furthermore, the luminescence intensity could be effected by antenna effect, and interaction of guests with metals and ligands, which make the luminescence property diversity and are seldom in other luminescence materials.36-38 Moreover, lanthanides have similar atomic radius and coordination characteristics, and polymetallic lanthanide MOFs could be easily synthesized by one pot.39 Those MOFs own more than one emission centers without additional materials, and are potential ratio luminescence sensors. Ratiometric luminescence sensors are self-calibrated, and could eliminate many obstacles of the conventional ones, which are usually based on single luminescence intensity, and whose accuracy is susceptible to some inevitable factors during the operation, such as probe concentration, source instability, temperature and so on.30,40,41
Designing and synthesizing multifunction materials is what scientists have been pursuing. And to our knowledge, there is few report about dual function luminescence ratiometric detector.
Based on the above considerations, we synthesized two new Ln-MOFs, $\{\mathrm{[Eu_{2}L_{2}]bullet2N H_{2}(C H_{3})_{2}\bullet H_{2}O\}}$ 1 $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{4}\mathrm{L}{=}5.$ -(bis(4-carboxybenzyl)am-ino)-isophthalicacid) (Eu-MOF) and Gd-MOF that is isomorphic with Eu-MOF, and mixed lanthanide $\mathrm{Eu}_{0.02}\mathrm{Dy}_{0.18^{-}}\mathrm{MOF}$ , which could be served as a dual functional luminescence ratiometric sensor for water in bio-ethanol and temperature in wide temperature range. With addition of water into dry ethanol, the luminescence intensity of $\mathrm{Eu}_{0.02}\mathrm{Dy}_{0.18}$ -MOF at $416~\mathrm{nm}$ was enhanced, and that at $614\mathrm{nm}$ was gradually quenched, and their ratios show good relationship with water content in range of $0.10\%$ . Fortunately, the relationship could apply to the simulated bio-ethanol system and have very good recoveries of $91\%\%-107\%$ in range of $0.6\%$ .
Besides, when heating the MOF from $298\mathrm{K}$ to $373\mathrm{K}$ or from $150\mathrm{K}$ to $298\mathrm{K}$ , all the luminescence intensity decrease due to the thermal activation of non-radiative decay pathways.42,43 And luminescence intensity ratio of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\ {}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\rightarrow{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{2}$ $\mathrm{\Delta}614~\mathrm{nm}$ ) transition to $\mathrm{Dy^{3+}~^{4}F_{9/2}}\rightarrow\mathrm{^{6}H_{13/2}}$ $573\ \mathrm{nm})$ transition versus temperature show good linear relationship. More importantly, the luminescence thermometers own high accuracy, fairly good linear relationship, reversibility and recyclability.
## Experimental Section
## Synthesis of Eu / Gd -MOF
Eu /Gd - MOF was prepared in the same way with that of Dy-MOF which had been synthesized in previous work44. It was that $3~\mathrm{ml}$ distilled water solution with 0.2 mmol $\mathrm{Eu(NO_{3})_{3}{\bullet}6H_{2}O\ /\ G d(N O_{3})_{3}{\bullet}6H_{2}O,}$ 4 ml DMA with 0.1 mmol $_\mathrm{H_{4}L}$ , and three drops of concentrated nitric acid were transferred into a $25~\mathrm{ml}$ Teflon lined steel vessel. The mixture was kept in a oven at $130~^{\mathrm{{0}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and then cooled down to room temperature slowly. Colorless lamellar crystals suitable for X-ray diffraction were obtained by filtration for Eu-MOF, white powder was obtained for Gd-MOF. Anal. Calcd for $\mathrm{C}_{52}\mathrm{H}_{48}\mathrm{N}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{17}\mathrm{Eu}_{2}$ ( $\%$ ) C 47.82, H 3.68, N 4.29. Found $(\%)$ : C, 47.29, H, 3.57, N, 4.24. IR (KBr, $\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ ): 3450 (s), 3065 (s), 2928 (s), 2799 (s), 2363 (s), 1632 (s), 1545 (s), 1427 (s), 1290 (s), 1250 (s), 1170 (s), 1086 (s), 1022 (s), 993 (s), 960 (s), 891 (s), 856 (s), 785 (s), 763 (s), 716 (s), 690 (s), 667 (s), 637 (s), 590 (s), 463 (s).
## Synthesis of $\mathbf{Eu_{x}D y_{0.2-x}}$ -MOF
The $\mathrm{Eu_{x}D y_{0.2-x^{-M O F}}}$ were synthesized in a similar way, expect that x mmol $\mathrm{Eu(NO_{3})_{3}}{\bullet}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ , 0.2-x mmol $\mathrm{Dy(NO_{3})_{3}}{\bullet}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ instead of 0.2 mmol $\mathrm{Eu(NO_{3})_{3}}{\bullet}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were dissolved in $3~\mathrm{ml}$ distilled water. The molar ratios of $\mathrm{Eu/Dy}$ in the $\mathrm{Eu_{x}D y_{0.2-x^{-M O F}}}$ were confirmed by ICP, and the real molar ratio of lanthanide ions are comparable with initial amounts (Table S3).
## Detection of water content in dry ethanol and model fuel ethanol
$3\ \mathrm{mg}$ MOFs were put into $10\mathrm{~ml~}$ dry ethanol, and the mixture was sonicated for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ to form suspension liquid. A certain amount of water was gradually added to the suspension liquid and whirled for 10s, then luminescence measurements were taken on the Edinburgh Instruments FLS920P fluorescence spectrometer.
The model fuel ethanol was prepared according to National standards of the People's Republic of China (GB 18350-2013) about denatured fuel ethanol. $0.73~\mathrm{\mg}$ anhydrous sodium sulfide, $0.13~\mathrm{{\mg}}$ anhydrous calcium chloride, and $50~\upmu\upmu\upmu$ methanol were dispersed in $9.95~\mathrm{ml}$ dry ethanol. Then luminescence experiments were carried out in the same way with in dry ethanol.
## Sensing of temperature
The synthesized $\mathrm{Eu}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Dy}_{0.2-\mathrm{x}}$ -MOF was encapsulated into the sample tank of TAP-02 high temperature fluorescent controller during $293\mathrm{~K~}$ to $373\mathrm{K}$ , and in ARS Optical Cryostat (4 K-300 K) during $150\mathrm{K}$ to $298\mathrm{K}$ . Before measurement temperature-dependent luminescence spectra, the sample was heated to a certain temperature and then kept for 30 minutes.
## Results and discussion
## Crystal structure of Eu – MOF
Eu-MOF, Gd-MOF and $\mathrm{Eu}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Dy}_{0.2-\mathrm{x}}.$ -MOF are isomorphic with Dy-MOF, which could be testified by single-crystal and $X\mathrm{-}$ ray diffraction analysis (Figure 1, Figure S1). Taking the Eu-MOF as example, as show in Figure 1a, every europium ion is also surrounded by eight oxygen atoms from six different ligands, and two of them form a binuclear unit bridged by four carboxyls from four different ligands. And every ligand connects to six metal atoms (Figure 1b). And the MOF is lumpy and micron seen from microscope after grinding it to powder(Figure S2).
## Framework stability
The TGA results show that the MOFs could keep stable up to $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S3), and after heating the $\mathrm{Eu}_{0.02}\mathrm{Dy}_{0.18}$ -MOF to $373\mathrm{~K~}$ and then cooling to $150~\mathrm{K}$ , it’s structure is intact (Figure S4). After immersing the $\mathrm{Eu}_{0.02}\mathrm{Dy}_{0.18}$ -MOF into water and ethanol for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ , the XRD spectrum has no change, so the compound is water and ethanol stability (Figure S5).
## Photoluminescence Properties
## Detection of water content in ethanol and AEHC
The excellent water and ethanol stability of the $\mathrm{Eu}_{0.02}\mathrm{Dy}_{0.18^{-}}$ MOF inspired us to study its fluorescent property in these solvents. We found that after dispersing $\mathrm{Eu}_{0.02}\mathrm{Dy}_{0.18}$ -MOF into water, the emission peak at $614~\mathrm{nm}$ disappeared, and that at $416\mathrm{{nm}}$ enhanced strongly, which was much different from that in ethanol (Figure S7), so the mixed MOF may be used to detect water in ethanol. To validate the efficiency of $\mathrm{Eu}_{0.02}\mathrm{Dy}_{0.18}$ -MOF sensing water in ethanol, different percentage water was added into $10\ \mathrm{ml}$ dry ethanol suspension, and then monitored the emission spectra. As shown in Figure 3a Figure S8, the luminescence intensity of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ at $614\mathrm{nm}$ gradually decreased, and the emission intensity of ligand at 416 nm increased, while the emission intensity of $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ at $480\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $573~\mathrm{nm}$ also increases simultaneously. When volume percent is from $0\%$ to $10\%$ , the relative ratio of the two emission intensity $(\mathrm{I}_{416}/\mathrm{I}_{614})$ versus water content showed best linear relation (Figure S9) and could fit well with the equation 1 (Figure 3b), which suggested that the MOF may be an excellent ratio detector for water content in ethanol.
$$
{\bf{y}}={\bf{b}}+{{\bf{A}}_{1}}^{*}(1-\exp(-\bf{x}/t_{1}))+{{\bf{A}}_{2}}^{*}(1-\exp(-\bf{x}/t_{2})){\bf{R}}^{2}=0.99
$$
The lowest detection limit for water was calculated to be $0.10\%$ according to 3δ/slope, which was much lower than that in AEHC (hydrated ethyl alcohol fuel, $0.8\%$ in China, $1.0\%$ in the United States, $4.9\%$ in Brazilian). To further explore the practical sensing feasibility of this ratiometric prober, the model system of AEHC spiked with different volumes water was studied with the MOF as luminescence sensor. The results were listed in Table 1. And the satisfying recoveries were $90\%-107\%$ in the range of $0\%$ - $6\%$ , which also indicated that coexisting substances $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{S}^{2\cdot}$ , methanol, Cl-) have negligible effect on the detection of water content in the AEHC.
## Water detection mechanism of $\mathbf{Eu}_{0.02}\mathbf{Dy}_{0.18}.$ -MOF
Based on the change of luminescence intensities(Figure 3a), we speculated the mechanism of sensing water may be that with the addition of water, interaction may cause between the MOF and water molecules, which suppress energy transfer from ligands to $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , and quench its luminescence intensity. However, this process has negligible influence on the $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ . In order to validate this hypothesis, we measured the lifetimes of as-synthesized $\mathrm{Eu}_{0.02}\mathrm{Dy}_{0.18}$ -MOF and that after immersing in water(Figure S11). The results were listed in Table S4, and they showed that the lifetimes of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ decrease greatly in wet sample, but that of $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ have no change, which are consistent with our speculation.
## Sensing of temperature
We also investigated temperature dependent luminescence performance of the $\mathrm{Eu}_{0.02}\mathrm{Dy}_{0.18^{-}}\mathrm{MOF}$ in wide temperature range $293\mathrm{~K~}$ to $373\mathrm{~K~}$ , and $150~\mathrm{K}$ to $298\ \mathrm{K},$ ) to get a tentative evaluation on the sensing performance of this self-calibrated luminescent thermometer. As shown in Figure 4a, the emission intensity of both $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ decrease as the temperature increases from $298\mathrm{~K~}$ to $373\mathrm{~K~}$ , And the emission intensity of $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ decreases much slowly than that of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , and the relative intensity $\Delta$ $\scriptstyle(\Delta=\mathrm{I}_{614}/\mathrm{I}_{573})$ and temperature have good linear relationship, which could be fitted as a function of
$$
\mathrm{T}{=}2.90935{\scriptstyle\mathrm{-}0.00704^{\ast}}\mathrm{I}_{614}/\mathrm{I}_{573}
$$
with a correction coefficient of 0.992 (Figure 4b). Interestingly, when we reduced temperature from 373 K to $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , the luminescence intensity increases gradually again, and relative luminescence intensity could recovery to almost the same values with those during the heating process (Figure 4c), which shows that the luminescence thermometer may have good reversibility and recyclability. Herein, circulation experiments were also measured during heating and cooling processes between $293{\mathrm{~K~}}$ to $373\mathrm{~K~}$ . After five circles, the relative luminescence intensity kept stable (Figure 4d).
Moreover, heating and cooling the MOF during $150~\mathrm{K}$ and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , the emission intensity of both $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ have the same change tendency (Figure 5) with that at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and $373\mathrm{~K~}$ . The relationship between the relative intensity $\Delta$ $\scriptstyle\lambda(\Delta=\mathrm{I}_{614}/\mathrm{I}_{573})$ and temperature could be fitted as a function of
$$
\mathrm{T}{=}2.3823{\scriptstyle{-0.00441}}\mathrm{I}_{614}/\mathrm{I}_{573}
$$
