with a correction coefficient of 0.97(Figure 5a, b). Good reversibility and recyclability also obtained(Figure 5c, d). Besides, relative sensitivity (Sr) is also an important parameter to measure the quality of a thermometer, and the Sr is $0.256\ \%/\mathrm{K}$ at 150 K, $2.48~\%/\mathrm{K}$ at $373\mathrm{~K~}$ , which are higher than some mixed lanthanide MOFs.24,34,42,43,50-53 So $\mathrm{Eu}_{0.02}\mathrm{Dy}_{0.18^{-}}$ MOF is an excellent self-calibrated luminescent thermometer with high accuracy, well linear relationship, reversibility and recyclability in wide temperature range.
## Temperature sensing mechanism of Eu0.02Dy0.18-MOF
With rising the temperature from $150~\mathrm{K}$ to $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and $293\mathrm{~K~}$ to $373\mathrm{~K~}$ , both the emission intensity of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ in $\mathrm{Eu}_{0.02}\mathrm{Dy}_{0.18}$ -MOF was quenched due to the thermal activation of non-radiative decay pathways.42,43 However, the intensity of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ decline faster than that of $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ (Figure S12). In order to explore the reason, the lifetimes of $\mathrm{{}^{5}D}_{0}\rightarrow{}^{7}\mathrm{{F}}_{2}$ transition $(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+})$ in Eu-MOF and ${^4}\mathrm{F}_{9/2}\rightarrow{^6}\mathrm{H}_{13/2}$ $(\mathrm{D}\mathbf{y}^{3+})$ in Dy-MOF, and those in $\mathrm{Eu}_{0.02}\mathrm{Dy}_{0.18^{-}}\mathrm{MOF}$ at different temperature were measured (Figure S11, S13, S14). As shown in Table S4, Figure S15, the lifetime of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ in $\mathrm{Eu}_{0.02}\mathrm{Dy}_{0.18^{-}}$ MOF is less than in Eu-MOF, and decrease slowly at low temperature, and decrease drastically at high temperature with the temperature increase, and the lifetimes of $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ in $\mathrm{Eu_{0.02}D y_{0.18^{-}M O F}}$ is also smaller than in Dy-MOF, but it keep stable with the temperature change(Figure S16). So energy transfer between ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ may not occur.46,54 Besides, as mentioned in previous work,44 the $\mathbf{S}_{1}$ of $_\mathrm{H_{4}L}$ is $28406~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , $\mathrm{T_{1}~23474~c m^{-1}}$ obtained from the emission spectrum of Gd-MOF at 77K (Figure S18) and comparable with that from DEF calculation, ΔE $\mathrm{(T_{1}\mathrm{\overline{{{s}}}D_{0})}}$ ) $6000\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , and $\Delta\mathrm{E}$ $\mathrm{.E}\left(\mathrm{T}_{1}\mathrm{-}^{4}\mathrm{F}_{9/2}\right)2512\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . And based on the Latva rule,55- 57 if the energy difference between the triplet state of ligands and the excited lanthanide states is around $2500-4500~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , the ligands could transfer energy to metals more effectively. Thus energy transfer from $_\mathrm{H_{4}L}$ to $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ may be more effective, and it is also the possible reason that as the temperature increase, the emission intensity of $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ was quenched more slowly, and the lifetime kept stable.58
## Conclusion
In this study, Eu/Gd-MOFs and white emission bimetallic lanthanide metal-organic framework $\mathrm{Eu}_{0.02}\mathrm{Dy}_{0.18}.$ -MOF have been fabricated successfully. In the mixed MOFs, the lanthanides could be sensitized by the $_\mathrm{H_{4}L}$ , and form three luminescence centers combined with ligand. In addition, their different sensitivity towards water and temperature makes the MOF as a potential ratio sensor. With the addition of water to ethanol, the luminescence intensity at $614~\mathrm{{nm}}$ was gradually quenched and that at $416\mathrm{nm}$ was enhanced, and their ratios show good relationship with water content in range $0.10\%$ , which was also suitable for bio-ethanol with recovery of $91\%\%\%-107\ \%$ in range of. $0.6\%$ ; when heating the MOF from $293\mathrm{~K~}$ to $373\mathrm{~K~}$ or from $150~\mathrm{K}$ to $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , all the luminescence intensity decrease due to the thermal activation of non-radiative decay pathways. And ratios of luminescence intensity of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ (at 614 nm) and $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ (at $573\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) versus temperature show good linear relation with high accuracy, well reversibility and recyclability. Ratiometric luminescence sensors display obvious advantage over traditional ones, so developing multifunction ratio sensors may be more convenient and necessary.
